to india now where confirmed infections  and deaths in a devastating second wave  have dipped below record levels  but there's concern that many cases are  not being reported  particularly from a surge in vast rural  areas  these regions lack adequate testing and  health care infrastructure  dw's nimisha jaiswal visited a village  near the city of mirot in uttar pradesh  where there has been a spike in deaths  caused by fever  it has been an exhausting month for saya  chauhan  for weeks his son haseen was racked by  fever and troubled breathing  chauhan who raises cattle for a living  finally arranged a car to take his son  to the hospital in the city  has seen tested positive for the  coronavirus  four days later he was discharged not  because he was feeling better  but because they had already racked up a  bill of about 1500 euros  haseem a daily wager earns 5 euros a day  when he can find work he didn't improve  at all at the hospital  we somehow arranged an oxygen cylinder  at home with great difficulty  i had to borrow money from several  people we didn't have a rupee  but for your child you'll do anything  the family of 30 lives crammed together  in close quarters they have been lucky  that no one else was taken seriously ill  and even luckier to get a diagnosis  many are falling sick in this village  which lies a hundred kilometers east of  the national capital  but there is no testing here there are  no government health facilities in this  village  small private clinics like this one are  the only stop gap  and have fielded hundreds of complaints  or fever in the last month alone  they do not have the provisions to treat  the coronavirus  and refer patients to the city but  treatment there is expensive  and given the curfew it is very  difficult to even travel there  so neil kumar has a car and could afford  treatment for his father and siblings  he even arranged beds through a contact  he lost all three of them last week the  doctors  asked the family to arrange medicines  but they were sold counterfeit bottles  the overwhelmed hospital charged twice  as much for services  recounting the horrors of what he saw  kumar is skeptical about the number of  deaths being reported  in the state i kept getting calls from  the authorities for three days after my  father's death  asking after his health i told them he'd  passed away  i don't even know if his death was  counted in the statistics  i saw eight bodies carried out of the  hospital the day my father died  from my family without diagnosis  fever deaths are on the rise jameel  johan a social worker  describes what has been happening first  they have a fever  then they feel breathless many  eventually die  at home in this house someone died  in that house too johan says there are  50 fresh graves in this cemetery alone  residents say cases of fevers started  spiking after rallies were held here for  local elections last month  johan started making a list of deaths in  the village  the cause of death for most is fever and  breathlessness  almost none of these have been recorded  as coronavirus deaths  every day people are dying nobody has  been tested  we haven't been told anything about the  vaccine no one here has gotten it  no doctors have visited us for the last  year  this village has been relatively  untouched by the pandemic  now residents are terrified they fear  that as recorded cases flag in urban  india  the corona virus will continue to wreck  unseen havoc here  for more i'm now joined by our  correspondent misha jaiswal who filed  that report  namisha we're seeing official numbers at  least heading in the right direction  which is a good sign but it seems that  india's  covert 19 crisis has shifted from urban  centers  to the countryside what does that mean  for the country's fight against covert  rebecca it mainly means that the  optimism we can feel about  numbers dipping may be too early  especially as we're hearing more and  more reports much  like in the village that we visited that  there have been many fevers  and many deaths which have been  completely missed  by the coronaval statistics that we are  feeling good about right now  of course india's numbers continue to be  above 200  000 above 200 000 a day  which is also a significant number of  course and is the highest  globally many of the infections have now  shifted from major centers like delhi  and mumbai to other smaller cities  but even then these are the numbers that  do still get recorded  even in the cities many struggled to get  tested at the peak  of the search and now there are large  swaths of the country that are being  completely missed when it comes to  testing  and this definitely impacts india's  ability to respond to the virus  if it's completely been missed in so  many places  what is the government doing to respond  to this crisis  well the good news is that the crisis in  rural india  has been acknowledged prime minister  modi said that local leaders  as well as local health care workers  will play a large role  in fighting the crisis in rural india so  this has been taken note of by the  national government  however there are large holes in  healthcare infrastructure  as well as logistics which is not  something that you should be reacting to  as the crisis is developing much as what  we saw in the oxygen crisis in the  cities  it was a bit too late to be responding  at this point of time  so right now again there are these holes  within the rural healthcare  infrastructure  that need to be urgently addressed an  additional concern is that  governments like the uttar pradesh  government and the state that we visited  have actually cracked down on villagers  for seemingly telling lies to the media  instead of rushing to their aid which  also raises further concern  that these problems are not being  acknowledged the way they should be  what more then could be or should be  done  the focus rebecca seems to be on ramping  up india's vaccination drive  just today prime minister modi said that  we must take collective responsibility  to expand the vaccination drive however  there are significant challenges the  government says that there will be a  much  larger amount of doses available by july  but right now  when anecdotally when we look at  available doses it is still a fight to  book a slot  to get an appointment especially between  the 18 and 44  age limit right now in addition even  when these doses eventually become  available  there is a high amount of hesitancy  there are many rumors and disinformation  going around  that the vaccine may actually harm you  rather than help you all of this will  need to be urgently addressed  if that third wave has to be fought off  dw correspondent namisha jaiswal in  delhi thanks very much
 

